{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792702385,"nonce":"h5lXPrHSyWJr1iV4yyUVYsS2X58=","title":"Compiler: float remainder is a libm fmod call in every tier; add a shared fast path","message":"A floating-point Rem has no hardware instruction on x86-64 or AArch64. Cranelift has no frem opcode, so the backend declares libm fmod and calls it (call_libm_fmod in cranelift_backend.rs); LLVM's frem lowers to the same libcall. Measured on a loop of k % 7 over 100M iterations: Node 165 ms, zynode default tier 2,570 ms, --llvm 2,467 ms, so LLVM does not help.\n\nInterim fix (TypeScript frontend only, lower/bitwise.rs remainder): positive integer operands below 2^63 take an inline integer remainder, everything else keeps fmod. Same loop afterwards: default 432 ms, --llvm 170 ms. Pitfall found on the way: Cranelift's float-to-integer conversion traps out of range (the interpreter saturates), so the range test must precede the conversion.\n\nProposal: one shared HIR pass that expands a float Rem into a range-and-integrality test, an integer srem when it holds and fmod otherwise, so Python, Lua, ZynML and TypeScript all benefit in both tiers and the frontend expansion can go. Sign semantics of Rem (dividend's sign) already match; a floored variant stays frontend-specific. A further inline path for non-integral operands is possible with fma plus one correction step when the quotient is below 2^53.","files":null}]}